{pause up}
# Motivations

{.example}
```ocaml
val foo : int -> float
```

{pause}
What can `foo` do?

{pause}
- Delete every file you own? {pause}
- Open a socket to anywhere on the internet? {pause}
- `fork()` bomb your machine? {pause}

{pause}
The type says nothing. You cannot reason locally about a module.

{pause up}
## Doesn't Haskell solve this?

The `IO` monad forces you to mark effectful code. {pause} But it's
coarse-grained: printing to stdout and opening a socket get the
**same type**.

{pause up}
# Capability types

Inspired by [eio](https://roscidus.com/blog/blog/2023/04/26/lambda-capabilities/),
generalized with plain OCaml **object types**.

{pause}
{.example}
```ocaml
val bar :
  < Cap.fork; Cap.network; Cap.stdout; Cap.random; .. > ->
  int -> float
```

{pause}
Now the signature says everything `bar` might do — and nothing it
can't.

{pause up}
## Capabilities are abstract types

{.example}
```ocaml
module Process : sig
  type fork   (* abstract: can not be forged *)
  type exit
  type chdir
  ...
end

type fork = < fork : Process.fork >
```

{pause}
Row-typed object types combine them for free — no OCaml extension
needed.

{pause up}
## The only door in: `Cap.main`

{.example}
```ocaml
type all_caps =
  < fork; network; random; stdout; stdin; exit; chdir; ... >

val main : (all_caps -> 'a) -> 'a
```

{pause}
- Callable exactly once — checked dynamically. {pause}
- Hands your entry point **every** capability. {pause}
- The entry point then **downcasts** (`:>`) to give each callee only
  the subset it needs.

{pause up}
## Capability-aware wrappers

{.example}
```ocaml
let fork (caps : < Cap.fork; .. >) =
  let _ = caps#fork in
  Unix.fork

let chdir (caps : < Cap.chdir; .. >) =
  let _ = caps#chdir in
  Unix.chdir
```

{pause}
`CapUnix`, `CapSys`, `CapStdlib`, `CapRandom`, ... wrap the standard
library one dangerous function at a time.

{pause up}
# Capability rules

`Cap.ml` alone doesn't stop anyone from calling `Unix.fork` directly
instead of `CapUnix.fork`.

{pause}
Enforce it with a Semgrep **capability rule**:

{pause}
{.example}
```json
{
  id: 'use-caps',
  match: { any: ['Unix.fork', 'Unix.wait', ...] },
  languages: ['ocaml'],
  severity: 'ERROR',
  message: |||
    Do not use Sys.xxx or Unix.xxx directly.
    Use CapSys or CapUnix and pass the required capability.
  |||,
}
```

{pause up}
## Going one step further

{.example}
```ocaml
(* TCB.ml, shadows Stdlib *)
let open_in = ()
let open_out = ()
let exit = ()
module Obj = struct end   (* no Obj.magic *)
```

{pause}
`-open TCB` still opens `Stdlib` underneath, so `TCB.ml` reuses the
same names and retypes them to `()` — killing the dangerous bindings
outright, everywhere, for free.

{pause up}
# Experience report

Rolled out on three real codebases:

{pause}
- **osemgrep** — the OCaml core of Semgrep itself {pause}
- the **mmm** browser {pause}
- several **Plan 9** systems programs ported to OCaml in **XiX**: a
  build system, a shell, a windowing system, a toolchain {pause}

{pause}
Each time: a handful of capability annotations plus a handful of
Semgrep rules was enough to enforce capabilities across the whole
codebase.

{pause up}
## A concrete win

{.block title="Found deep in the call stack"}
While migrating Semgrep, the capability rules flagged a call to the
external `uname` program via `Unix.open_process_in` — added by a
junior developer, missed in code review, and a performance
bottleneck.

{pause up}
## Why reviewers like it

Once a codebase is migrated, a quick-and-dirty hack needs a new
capability threaded through the **whole call chain** — much harder to
sneak past review.

{pause up}
## And for AI coding agents

{pause}
- They pick up the capability discipline instantly, no coaching
  needed. {pause}
- A reviewer can trust the *interface*: if it needs no dangerous
  capability, the type system guarantees the implementation can't use
  one either. {pause}
- Stronger types become a **sandbox** around generated code, not just
  documentation.

{pause up}
